# Local Vercel CLI state and dependency/build output. Nothing in this
# repo is built ahead of deployment -- public/ is served as-is and api/
# is bundled by Vercel -- so anything below only ever appears from
# running the CLI or npm locally.
.vercel/
node_modules/
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# The extracted voice-line corpus stays local. The project's whole legal
# position is that it distributes no game content -- the plan puts it as
# "it never touches or redistributes Activision's assets/code itself", and
# LICENSE 6 says the same. 1,848 clips lifted off a retail disc contradicts
# that, and the wiki comparison does not hold at this scale: the wiki hosts a
# handful of catchphrases as citations, not most of a game's voice track.
#
# Nothing is lost. branding/voicelines/FINDINGS.md documents the igArchive v4
# layout, the DSP-ADPCM codec and the extraction method, so anyone with their
# own disc can regenerate the whole set. The 14 short catchphrases the site
# actually plays stay in public/audio as citations.
branding/voicelines/*/

# Working intermediates, not deliverables
branding/icon/out/
branding/icon/out-tight/
# Added by the Vercel CLI when the project was linked. .env* is broader than
# the .env / .env.* above on purpose: `vercel env pull` writes .env.local and
# whatever name you give it, and none of those may ever be committed.
.vercel
.env*
